              1773.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "paragraph_title",
          "block_content": "Using Like Bases to Solve Exponential Equations",
          "block_bbox": [
            208,
            1810,
            1251,
            1865
          ],
          "block_id": 12,
          "block_order": 10,
          "group_id": 12,
          "block_polygon_points": [
            [
              208.0,
              1810.0
            ],
            [
              1251.0,
              1810.0
            ],
            [
              1251.0,
              1865.0
            ],
            [
              208.0,
              1865.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "The first technique involves two functions with like bases. Recall that the one-to-one property of exponential functions tells us that, for any real numbers $b, S$, and $T$, where $b > 0$, $b \\neq 1$, $b^{S} = b^{T}$ if and only if $S = T$.",
          "block_bbox": [
            206,
            1885,
            2332,
            1991
          ],
          "block_id": 13,
          "block_order": 11,
          "group_id": 13,
          "block_polygon_points": [
            [
              206.0,
              1885.0
            ],
            [
              2332.0,
              1885.0
            ],
            [
              2332.0,
              1991.0
            ],
            [
              206.0,
              1991.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "In other words, when an exponential equation has the same base on each side, the exponents must be equal. This also applies when the exponents are algebraic expressions. Therefore, we can solve many exponential equations by using the rules of exponents to rewrite each side as a power with the same base. Then, we use the fact that exponential functions are one-to-one to set the exponents equal to one another, and solve for the unknown.",
          "block_bbox": [
            207,
            2012,
            2336,
            2222
          ],
          "block_id": 14,
          "block_order": 12,
          "group_id": 14,
          "block_polygon_points": [
            [
              207.0,
              2012.0
            ],
            [
              2336.0,
              2012.0
            ],
            [
              2336.0,
              2222.0
            ],
            [
              207.0,
              2222.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "For example, consider the equation  $ 3^{4x-7} = \\frac{3^{2x}}{3} $. To solve for x, we use the division property of exponents to rewrite the right side so that both sides have the common base, 3. Then we apply the one-to-one property of exponents by setting the exponents equal to one another and solving for x:",
          "block_bbox": [
            207,
            2248,
            2335,
            2449
          ],
          "block_id": 15,
          "block_order": 13,
          "group_id": 15,
          "block_polygon_points": [
